{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>On the tenth of the month.</b> The Egyptian Passover was taken on the tenth. And when the firstborn of Egypt saw this, they said to Israel: 'What is this to you?' They said to them: 'It is a Passover — that HaShem will pass over the houses of the children of Israel in Egypt and slay the firstborn among the Egyptians.' And they said to them: 'Send Israel away, for you are causing us to die!' They refused, and the firstborn killed many of them. And that is what is written: למכה מצרים בבכוריהם — 'to him who smote Egypt through their firstborn.' It does not say 'the firstborn of Egypt,' but 'Egypt through their firstborn' — for the firstborn smote them themselves. And furthermore, it is said in the Midrash: for when they took the Passover on the tenth of the month, it was the Sabbath — for we hold that the Egyptian Passover was on the fifth day of the week, as is written in tractate Shabbat, chapter רבי עקיבא; if so, its tenth was on the Sabbath before it. And when the Egyptians saw them taking the Paschal lambs to slaughter, they gathered against them to kill them, for the Egyptians feared sheep. And the Holy One, blessed be He, performed a miracle for them and they were saved. Therefore it is called 'the Great Sabbath' (Shabbat HaGadol) because of the greatness of the miracle. But in any case, one must give a reason why it was taken from the tenth. Rashi says: so that they would be occupied with circumcision and with the blood of the Passover for four days. And they ask: why four days? And Rabbi Yitzchak of Évreux says: so that they would circumcise and wait three days after the circumcision before setting out on the journey."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:59.550181109Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}